  That answer has a concrete consequence I want to name now: it means the task model gets designed for a server it
  doesn't have yet. Specifically, tasks get stable IDs rather than array indices, and every change gets a timestamp and
  an actor field — even though the actor is always "me" today. Those two things are nearly free to add now and genuinely
  painful to retrofit once there's data in the wild. Everything else stays local and simple.

  Now, what should actually fire a notification. This defines the task model, because each trigger requires a field to
  exist:

  - [1mOverdue[0m needs a [38;5;153mdueDate[39m. Cheap — computable on page load, no timer.
  - [1mDue soon[0m needs [38;5;153mdueDate[39m plus a threshold, [3mand[0m a timer while the page is open, since "soon" becomes true while you're
    sitting there.
  - [1mStale[0m needs [38;5;153mupdatedAt[39m. Cheap, computable on load.
  - [1mStatus changed[0m needs a change log — the most useful one later for multi-user, and the only one that's near-useless
    today, since you're the one making the changes.

  The split that matters: triggers computable [1mon load[0m are nearly free. Triggers that fire [1mwhile the page is open[0m need a
  scheduler and a "don't tell me this twice" record. That's the real complexity line, not the number of trigger types.
